WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.833
Why are we all so obsessed with the Joker?

00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:07.715
[gunshot]

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.090
Joker: Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.070
We are tonight's entertainment.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:18.903
Narrator: There are, of course,

00:00:18.903 --> 00:00:20.780
several explanations for this.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.040
For one, he is one of the oldest villains

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.950
in comic book history,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.390
his first appearance dating
all the way back in 1940,

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.303
with "Batman" issue No. 1.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.280
But I think what really
propelled the character to fame

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.910
was in its various incarnations
throughout history.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.010
From comics to television,
movies, video games,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.760
and animation, the Joker is probably one

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.950
of the most readapted villains in history.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.589
And, understandably, the question

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:52.400
of who did it best is an ongoing debate.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.520
But I think it's an unfair question,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.550
considering that every
incarnation of the character,

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.370
more specifically the four
different types of Joker

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.843
we've seen throughout history,

00:01:01.843 --> 00:01:05.230
are all entirely different characters.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.270
But without even just one
of them, the Joker would not

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.561
have evolved into the perfect
villain we know today.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.297
[evil laughing]

00:01:22.851 --> 00:01:25.323
Joker: Come, my comic cohorts in crime!

00:01:27.068 --> 00:01:28.810
Narrator: The Joker's
initial appearance in comics

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.570
was very different from
the Joker we know today.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.493
His appearance in "Batman" No. 1

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:36.835
portrayed him as a smart
and capable criminal

00:01:36.835 --> 00:01:39.351
driven primarily by greed,

00:01:39.351 --> 00:01:43.490
committing a series of murders
to steal precious jewels.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.060
Perhaps the most vital trait
that was first established

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.570
was his penchant for theatricality,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.843
announcing his crime via radio
before they even happened.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.210
This would in time become a
trope that would be used again.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.000
Smylex model: Love that Joker.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.130
Narrator: And again.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.740
Joker: Every day he
doesn't, people will die.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.450
Narrator: He was smart,
cunning, and ruthless.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.740
But despite it all, the Joker was still

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.906
just another criminal,

00:02:08.906 --> 00:02:11.900
albeit with an unusual sense of humor.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.550
Several critics have viewed this Joker

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.970
as a perfect example of
the trickster archetype,

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.470
one of the many archetypes devised

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.150
by psychoanalyst Carl Jung.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.988
In literature, these are characters

00:02:22.988 --> 00:02:25.146
who show high levels of intelligence,

00:02:25.146 --> 00:02:26.887
use their mind to play tricks,

00:02:26.887 --> 00:02:29.700
or disobey any established rules.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.330
Another great example of
this archetype is Loki

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.327
from the Norse mythology and
subsequently the Marvel comics,

00:02:36.327 --> 00:02:38.836
who, like the Joker, treads a thin line

00:02:38.836 --> 00:02:43.110
between malicious violence
and harmless humor.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.740
When the "silver age" of
comics hit in the '50s,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.360
DC began toning down
the evil side of Joker

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.150
to market their books for children

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.920
and to avoid strict censorship.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.080
The 1942 story "The Joker
Walks the Last Mile"

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.230
became a turning point that transformed

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.860
the unusual, menacing
serial killer into a joke.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.370
And Cesar Romero's
portrayal of the character

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.350
in the 1960s television series "Batman"

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.570
only solidified this image,
depicting the character

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:15.110
as an intelligent yet
mostly harmless prankster

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.333
with an unusual set of gadgets.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.460
As the show's popularity
waned, so did the Joker,

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.760
until in 1973, the character made a return

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.609
in an almost unrecognizable form.

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:30.520
Harley Quinn: Presenting
that caliph of clowns,

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:35.481
that mogul of mountebanks,
the one and only Joker!

00:03:36.373 --> 00:03:39.040
[evil laughing]

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.570
Narrator: Under writer Dennis O'Neil,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.950
the "Batman" comics began taking
a darker and grittier turn,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.050
and with it, the villains.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:53.140
"Batman" No. 251 signified
the return of the Joker

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.723
as a criminal and a serial killer.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.950
It was at this time that
Joker truly turned mad.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.220
O'Neil and the writers
who came after him began

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.705
focusing on Joker's
insanity, and with it came

00:04:06.705 --> 00:04:09.613
the creation of the
landmark Arkham Asylum.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.860
Joker was no longer scary because he was

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.140
a ruthless criminal, but because he was

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.663
an insane and unpredictable one.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.080
Writer Steve Englehart's
arc "The Laughing Fish"

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:25.670
was an iconic story that
exemplified this insanity,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.860
showing the Joker
poisoning the water supply

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.800
to make the fish look like him

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.700
so he could copyright
and profit off of them.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.233
The same story was also
adapted in the '90s'

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:37.108
"Batman: The Animated Series,"

00:04:37.108 --> 00:04:39.199
expertly voiced by Mark Hamill,

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:43.380
whose Joker was heavily inspired
by the Joker of the '70s.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.030
It's a ridiculous
premise, but nonetheless,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.560
the portrayal of the Joker
is absolutely terrifying.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.420
Joker: I think I'll start
using my toxin on cattle!

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.062
Joker Burgers! Ha, talk
about a happy meal.

00:04:58.062 --> 00:04:59.360
[laughing hysterically]

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.290
Narrator: The era signified a huge shift

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.610
in the characterization of the Joker,

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.830
but the transformation
was far from complete.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:10.570
All of this changed with just one work

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:13.390
that would revolutionize
the idea of the Joker

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:15.565
for years to come:

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.706
Alan Moore's "The Killing Joke."

00:05:18.706 --> 00:05:21.846
[laughing maniacally]

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.280
Published during the "dark age" of comics,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.120
when stories became even
darker and more mature

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.718
to target adult audiences,
"The Killing Joke"

00:05:32.718 --> 00:05:35.090
was a revolutionary work for the character

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.220
for a number of reasons.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.890
First, it established the idea

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.590
that the Joker has no specific origin.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.970
Contrary to popular
belief, "The Killing Joke"

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.650
does not tell a definitive
origin story for the Joker,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.070
but only a possible one
inspired by a story that was

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.953
already told years ago in
"Detective Comics" No. 168.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:00.647
What is definitive is
Joker's view on his past.

00:06:00.647 --> 00:06:03.057
Joker: Sometimes I remember it one way,

00:06:03.057 --> 00:06:05.086
sometimes another.

00:06:05.086 --> 00:06:07.250
If I'm going to have a past,

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:10.220
I prefer it to be multiple choice.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.080
Narrator: This turned the
Joker from an evil man

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.450
to an idea, an embodiment of his belief.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.920
And with it, the Joker became more vocal

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.540
about what he believes in,
such as his frightening

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.910
observation that all it takes for someone

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:26.830
to become like him is just...

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.096
Joker: One bad day.

00:06:29.096 --> 00:06:31.629
That's how far the world
is from where I am,

00:06:31.629 --> 00:06:34.315
just one bad day.

00:06:34.315 --> 00:06:35.730
Narrator: The second revolutionary aspect

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.470
was in the novel's interpretation

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.120
of Joker's relationship with Batman.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:44.000
Moore saw Batman and Joker as
a mirror image of each other,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.560
both creations of a
random and tragic event

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.450
that led to an alternate identity

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.760
and opposing views of the world.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.464
This meant that their fights
were no longer just physical,

00:06:55.464 --> 00:06:58.087
but really a philosophical one.

00:06:58.087 --> 00:07:01.200
"The Killing Joke" opened the
floodgate to other stories

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.683
that began exploring the
Joker as a philosopher.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:08.640
It was also what heavily
inspired Jack Nicholson's iconic

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.750
portrayal of the character
in Tim Burton's "Batman."

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:14.728
Joker: As you can see, I'm a lot happier.

00:07:14.728 --> 00:07:16.640
Narrator: His Joker was
really a combination

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.910
of all the Jokers before him.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.900
The campiness of the '60s,
the insanity of the '70s,

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.560
and the philosophical
implication of the modern era.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.530
This is perhaps why Nicholson's
portrayal was so iconic,

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.900
as it provided the most
comprehensive look at the character

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.383
that has drastically
evolved over the years.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.900
Until 2008, when director
Christopher Nolan

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.330
and Heath Ledger proved
that Joker's transformation

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.398
was far from over.

00:07:47.398 --> 00:07:49.815
[sirens blaring]

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:54.750
Ledger's portrayal of the Joker was

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.720
quite unlike anything we've seen.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.660
There were characteristics that
clearly made him the Joker,

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.070
like his pension for theatricality,

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:07.367
the multiple origin stories,
and a macabre sense of humor.

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:09.530
Joker: I don't wanna kill you.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.520
Narrator: But if
Nicholson's Joker was more

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.850
of a performance artist,
Ledger's was that of a terrorist

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.480
who exploited the modern fear of political

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.630
and philosophical extremism
in the post-9/11 America.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:26.050
He was an anarchist who
not only refused to follow

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:30.110
the established order, but
actively seeked to destroy it,

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.710
introducing chaos to
the political landscape

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.161
of Gotham as its...

00:08:34.161 --> 00:08:36.430
Joker: I'm an agent of chaos.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.290
Narrator: Nolan also provided
a specific philosophy

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:42.100
to ground the character: nihilism.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.883
A philosophical belief that denies

00:08:43.883 --> 00:08:47.010
all meaningful values in life.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.771
One critic compared the Joker
to what Friedrich Nietzsche

00:08:49.771 --> 00:08:53.660
ironically called Übermensch, or Superman,

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.620
a being that independently
decides on what's right or wrong

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.118
and asserts their own
belief into the world.

00:09:00.118 --> 00:09:05.110
Joker: It's not about money;
it's about sending a message.

00:09:06.102 --> 00:09:08.410
Narrator: Others also noticed
that this interpretation

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.070
of the Batman and the
Joker's relationship mirrored

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:15.100
the Nietzschean philosophy of
the master and slave morality,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:18.030
Joker being the master
morality that creates

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.890
and defines its own rules
without the approval of others

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:25.130
and considers what
benefits them to be good,

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.270
while Batman serving as the slave morality

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.442
that is bound by existing laws

00:09:30.442 --> 00:09:33.170
and distinguishes right from wrong,

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:38.090
the two in constant battle
for what each finds just.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.680
Ledger's Joker was scary,
because, like "The Killing Joke,"

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.140
it embodied an idea of chaos
and anarchy that couldn't be...

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.498
Alfred Pennyworth:
Bought, bullied, reasoned,

00:09:48.498 --> 00:09:49.681
or negotiated with.

00:09:49.681 --> 00:09:53.246
Narrator: An entity that Batman
can't just win physically.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:58.280
Joker's transformation is an ongoing one.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:01.277
The most recent adaptation,
by Joaquin Phoenix,

00:10:01.277 --> 00:10:03.027
seems to be more political,

00:10:03.027 --> 00:10:06.144
representing him as a Marxist figure.

00:10:06.144 --> 00:10:08.370
And in recent comics,
Scott Snyder portrayed

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.050
the Joker as an almost demonic figure,

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:15.010
an evil as old as the
city of Gotham itself.

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:16.680
And perhaps this is why we're

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.330
all so obsessed with the Joker.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.670
It's a character that constantly changes

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:25.210
and evolves to reflect our various fears.

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:27.250
To borrow the mind of Nietzsche,

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:31.010
it's not the abyss you're
staring into that scares you,

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.510
but the abyss staring back,

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:38.070
with a big, clown-like smile.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:43.070
Joker: See, madness, as
you know, is like gravity.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.939
All it takes is a little push.

00:10:45.939 --> 00:10:49.606
[Joker laughing maniacally]

